{"id":"32ab22cb-2a2e-43fc-9d95-6ad40ddd145a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022988,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"]\\n    \\n    # Build node map and children map\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    \\n    # Initial retained set and symbol counts\\n    retained = set(node[\\\"id\\\"] for node in nodes)\\n    symbol_count = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    # Check feasibility: all required symbols must be present\\n    for s in required_symbols:\\n        if symbol_count.get(s, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Required symbol missing\\\")\\n    \\n    # Build candidate list: removable, not root, not required symbol\\n    root_id = None\\n    candidates = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n        candidates.append((node[\\\"depth\\\"], nid, node))\\n    \\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n    \\n    removed_order = []\\n    for depth, nid, node in candidates:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        # Must have no retained children\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        # Tentatively remove: check required symbols\\n        sym = node[\\\"symbol\\\"]\\n        new_count = symbol_count[sym] - 1\\n        if sym in required_symbols and new_count == 0:\\n            continue\\n        # Proceed with removal\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_count[sym] = new_count\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    \\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_list\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that given a payload with nodes and required_symbols, returns removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction (Perses style). Key points:\n\n- Input: nodes list with id, parent (id or null), symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float)\n- required_symbols: list of symbols that must be present in the retained tree.\n- We need to decide which nodes to remove (top-down? depth-first? The instruction: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests we process nodes in order of decreasing depth, i.e., deepest first. Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (or any stable order). \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This implies we try to remove nodes if removable and after removal the set of symbols in the retained tree still covers required_symbols. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That last part: \"a node with retained children\" means if a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove it? In Perses, you can remove a node only if its subtree is fully removed? Actually, in parse-tree reduction, you typically replace a subtree with a non-terminal or remove it. But here it says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests you cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. So we must ensure that before removing a node, all its children are already removed (or it has no children). That aligns with processing deepest first: if we try to remove a node, its children (if any) are deeper and would have been considered first; if they were not removed (retained), then the parent cannot be removed. So we must track which nodes are retained.\n\nAdditionally: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after tentatively removing a node (and possibly its subtree), we need to check that all required symbols are still present somewhere in the retained tree. Since we are processing deepest first and removing nodes when possible, we should simulate removal and check.\n\nAlso: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for nodes at the same depth, we process them in order of node ID (string) maybe lexicographically? Likely ascending.\n\nThe output: removed_node_ids (list of IDs in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted? \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe alphabetically? Or by depth? Probably sorted lexicographically or by id). The instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs\", so we sort them. Also \"the actual removal order\" meaning the order in which nodes were definitely removed. So removed_node_ids should be in order of removal.\n\nWe need to implement algorithm:\n\n1. Build a mapping from node id to node dict.\n2. Build children mapping: for each node, find children from parent references.\n3. Start with all nodes initially retained (by default no removals). We'll maintain a set of retained IDs.\n4. Sort candidate removable nodes (excluding root, required symbols? Actually root and nodes with required symbols are not removable? The instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we should not even consider removing nodes that are root or have symbol in required_symbols. Also nodes with retained children: but initially all children are retained, so only leaf nodes can be considered initially.\n5. Process nodes in order: depth descending, then id ascending.\n6. For each node:\n   - Check if it is currently retained.\n   - Check if it is removable (node[\"removable\"] == True).\n   - Check if it is root? root has parent null; we should not remove root.\n   - Check if its symbol is in required_symbols? Then cannot remove.\n   - Check if any of its children are retained (i.e., still in retained set). If yes, skip (cannot remove parent before children).\n   - Tentatively: remove the node (and its entire subtree? Actually removing a node in Perses means removing the entire subtree rooted at that node. In our simulation, if we remove a node, we remove it and all descendants. But we are processing deepest first, so when we consider a node, its children may have already been removed or not. But if any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So we only consider nodes that have no retained children. When we decide to remove a node, we remove it and all its descendants (which are already removed if we processed deeper first). But we must be careful: if a descendant was previously removed, it's already out. Removing the node itself will also remove any descendants that might still be retained (if we didn't process deeper? But we process deepest first, so by the time we consider a node, all its descendants have already been considered; if any were retained, we can't remove parent. So if children are all removed, then removing parent just removes the parent node itself; descendants are already gone. So we can simply remove the node from retained set, and also remove its descendants? But they are already gone. So effectively, removing a node means we mark it as removed. And we need to check that after removing this node, the set of symbols still covers required_symbols. That includes symbols from other parts of the tree not removed. So we need to compute the current set of symbols from retained nodes. We could maintain a symbol count or set. Since we remove nodes one by one, we can update a symbol count map.\n\n7. Important: The condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before actually removing, we check if required_symbols are satisfied in the current retained set after the removal of that node. If yes, we remove it and record its id in removed_node_ids (in order). If not, we keep it.\n\n8. At the end, retained_node_ids is the set of node IDs that were never removed. We sort them (maybe by id, alphanumerically). removed_node_ids list in order of removal.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that returns {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}.\n\nWe'll also handle error cases: maybe check that the input has required fields, but we can assume valid.\n\nThe payload has _case_nonce but we ignore.\n\nImplementation steps in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    # Build node map\n    node_map = {node[\"id\"]: node for node in nodes}\n    # Build children lists\n    children_map = {node[\"id\"]: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node.get(\"parent\")\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map[parent].append(node[\"id\"])\n    # Initially all nodes retained\n    retained = set(node[\"id\"] for node in nodes)\n    # Symbol count: maintain count of each symbol among retained nodes\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Check initial feasibility: required symbols must be present\n    for s in required_symbols:\n        if symbol_count.get(s, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Required symbol missing\")\n    # Process nodes in order: depth descending, then id ascending\n    # Create list of (depth, id, node) for all nodes that are removable, not root, not required symbol\n    candidates = []\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = node[\"id\"]\n            continue\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        candidates.append((node[\"depth\"], node[\"id\"], node))\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n    removed_order = []\n    for depth, nid, node in candidates:\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check if any children are retained\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children_map.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Tentatively remove: compute new symbol counts\n        # We need to subtract symbol of this node and all descendants? But descendants are already removed if we processed deeper first? Actually it's possible that a descendant was not removed because it was not removable or required, but then we cannot remove this node because child retained. So if we reach here, no retained children. But what about deeper descendants that are not direct children? If we have no retained children, then all children are removed; but children might have had retained grandchildren? Actually if a child is removed, its entire subtree is removed. So if no retained children, then the entire subtree is gone. So we only need to subtract the symbol of this node. But there could be descendants that were not removable and therefore retained, but then they would be retained children or deeper? Actually if a descendant is retained, then its parent chain must be retained (since parent can't be removed if child retained). So if a node has no retained children, then none of its descendants are retained. So safe to just subtract its own symbol.\n        sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n        # Check if after removal, all required symbols still have count >=1\n        new_count = symbol_count[sym] - 1\n        if new_count == 0 and sym in required_symbols:\n            # would lose required symbol\n            continue\n        # Also need to check for other required symbols? They are not affected because only this symbol changed.\n        # However, if removing this node removes a symbol that is required but there are other nodes with that symbol, it's fine.\n        # So we check: if sym in required_symbols and new_count == 0: skip.\n        # Actually we also need to ensure that all required symbols remain represented. If sym not required, then fine.\n        # So condition: if sym in required_symbols and new_count == 0: skip.\n        # But what about required symbols that are not this symbol? they are unchanged.\n        # So we can proceed.\n        # Remove node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_count[sym] = new_count\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    # After loop, retained set is final\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)  # sort by id (string)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_list}\n\nBut we need to be careful: The condition \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" is applied by our conditions: we skip root by not including, skip required symbols by not including, and skip if has retained children. So fine.\n\nAlso note: The root is always retained because we never consider it. Also required symbols are never removed. That matches.\n\nNow test with given fixtures: We can simulate mentally but trust.\n\nPotential edge cases: What if a removable node has descendants that are not removable but are required? Then it would have retained children, so not removed. So fine.\n\nWhat about the case where a node is removable, its symbol is not required, but removing it would cause a required symbol to disappear because that required symbol only appears in that node's subtree? But we already check: if the node's symbol is not required, we only check if removing it causes count of that symbol to go to zero and that symbol is required. But if the node's symbol is not required, but its subtree contains required symbols? However, we are processing deepest first, so if a required symbol is in a descendant, that descendant would already have been considered. If it was retained (because it is required symbol, so not removable), then the parent would have a retained child, so it would be skipped. So safe. If the required symbol is in a descendant that is removable? No, required symbol nodes are not removable per our candidates filter. So child would be retained. So parent not removed.\n\nThus algorithm seems correct.\n\nWe must implement solve(case) and return the dict. Also note: The function should not mutate case. We are not mutating.\n\nAlso need to handle potential missing fields? The payload should have nodes and required_symbols. We assume valid.\n\nNow produce the JSON envelope with key \"implementation\" and the Python code as a string. The code must be valid Python, no external imports, only standard library. Use only simple data structures.\n\nWe'll also include a check for feasibility at start: ensure required_symbols are present.\n\nNow write the Python code as a string.\n\nAlso note: The output format: removed_node_ids is in removal order, retained_node_ids sorted. We sort retained by id (string). That seems natural.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3355,"total_tokens":5527,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2751},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}